Subject: [PATCH] friends: resolve console identity per request, and report
 console presence
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Server-side half of the fix for the two Switch friends problems: friends who
never appear online, and every console adding friends as ONE account (the
project owner's friend code showing up on everybody's console). Full analysis
in FRIENDS-FIX.md.

WHY IT HAPPENED

A Switch presents a BAAS id_token carrying "sub" (the BAAS/NSA user id, per
console USER) and "bs:did" (the device account id, per console). nx-account has
to turn that into a Nextendo account on EVERY request, and the only tool it had
was /internal/pid-by-bsdid, which compares bs:did against a value DERIVED from
the account PID. That works only when the console adopts the id we minted — but
a console caches its device account in system save 8000000000000010 and presents
its OWN id forever. So a console provisioned before its account existed, or
provisioned while somebody else's account was linked, matches nothing, the
lookup 404s, and nx-account falls back to a process-wide "last authenticated
account" variable (the comment on internalPIDByBsDid says exactly that). Every
console then converges on one identity. Reissuing a friend code changes the
code, not the binding — which is what players observed.

Presence had a parallel gap: the table has a 90s TTL and only two writers (the
emulator fork, and a NEX game server for players inside that game). Nothing
reported a console that is simply on and online, and nx-account — which does see
the console's presence — had no way to write it, because /api/presence needs a
user bearer token a server-to-server caller does not have.

WHAT THIS COMMIT ADDS

Console identity bindings (nintendo_bindings.go, nintendo_bindings_store.go)
  * Account.NintendoBindings: the ids a console ACTUALLY presents, several per
    account (one per console/profile), with bound_at/last_seen and an operator
    label.
  * GET /internal/whoami?baas=&bsdid= — the single resolution entry point.
    Order: binding on baas -> binding on bs_did -> derived BaasID -> derived
    BsDid. There is deliberately NO fifth step: an unresolvable console gets
    404 and the caller must refuse it. That absent fallback IS the fix.
  * POST /internal/bind — records a binding, called by the console link flow
    with the ids from the token the console actually presented. This is what
    makes a console with a pre-existing device account work WITHOUT a factory
    reset: we learn its id instead of expecting it to adopt ours.
  * POST /internal/unbind — the recovery path for a console poisoned by the old
    fallback (unbind from the wrong account, bind to the right one).
  * A binding belongs to exactly ONE account: binding an id another account owns
    is refused with 409, never silently moved — a console quietly changing owner
    would be the same bug in a new shape.
  * O(1) resolution through an in-memory reverse index, replacing a full account
    scan on every console request (also a small denial-of-service surface).
  * Ids are canonicalised to %016x so the hex form from an id_token and the
    decimal form other paths use (/api/nsa) resolve to the same binding, with
    the "1000 is both" ambiguity resolved by length.
  * /internal/pid-by-bsdid now uses the same index, so it improves before
    nx-account changes at all, and logs an explicit line when it cannot resolve.

Console presence (console_presence.go, presence.go)
  * POST /internal/presence — a single-account presence write for an internal
    caller, which is what nx-account needs and did not have. status 0 clears the
    presence immediately, so "went offline" is instant instead of a TTL expiry.
  * noteConsoleOnline(): /internal/identity, /internal/pid-by-bsdid,
    /internal/whoami and /internal/login are called BY nx-account on the
    console's behalf, so reaching them is proof the console is on and connected.
    Each now publishes an online presence — this works with NO change on the
    nx-account side.
  * A stronger, fresher presence is never downgraded: a player in a game stays
    "playing" instead of being demoted by a friend-list poll.
  * The console presence has its own, longer TTL
    (NEXTENDO_CONSOLE_PRESENCE_TTL, default 5 min), because a console polls less
    often than a game reports and 90s would make friends flicker.
  * clearPresence() added to presence.go for the immediate-offline path.

NPLN bridge (npln_friends.go)
  * Publishes "favorite" per friend and a "blocked" array. Splatoon 3 reads
    both: without the flag every friend looks unstarred in game while the
    website shows the star, and an empty block list lets the game match a player
    with somebody they blocked.

WHAT nx-account STILL HAS TO DO (not public, specified in FRIENDS-FIX.md)
  Resolve with /internal/whoami using the ids from the id_token of the request
  being served; DELETE the global fallback and answer an auth error on 404;
  cache per (sub, bs:did) pair only; call /internal/bind at link time; call
  /internal/presence on the console's presence updates; and map each friend's
  presenceStatus into the BAAS friend object.

TESTS
  Explicit and derived resolution, 404-with-no-pid for an unknown console
  (the regression guard for the whole bug), binding exclusivity (409),
  unbind/rebind, hex vs decimal, two consoles keeping two distinct friend codes,
  presence published on resolution, "playing" not downgraded, immediate offline,
  unknown PID refused, and favorites/blocks in the NPLN bridge.

No existing route changes shape; everything added is a new /internal/* route or
a new field.
